      "tests": [
        "VNI_REQUIRE_MONGO=1 dotnet test --nologo from backend/ -> exit 0, 0 skipped: Domain 189, Application 178, Architecture 10, Infrastructure 108, Worker 13, Integration 188. 61 cases added.",
        "node scripts/check-docs.mjs exit 0 - re-verified by orchestrator, 13 collections",
        "check-generated-drift --mode=all exit 0"
      ],
      "negativeProof": "three, all red then green. 1) guard removed from the endpoint -> 3 integration tests red (Expected Conflict, Actual OK) while A_source_that_holds_the_right_publishes stayed GREEN, proving a gate rather than a constant refusal. 2) default-deny removed from Evaluate -> 2 domain + 1 application red. 3) hash comparison removed from Compare -> 1 domain + 1 infrastructure red, the latter end-to-end over a real temp file so it runs in CI without the gitignored content.",
      "artifacts": ["_workspace/workflow/agents/backend-content-rights.md"],
      "blocker": null,
      "nextDependency": "FS2.5 pilot publish path; FS1.5 shares contracts/openapi",
      "notes": "21 seeded entries (Cambridge 16-21, VOL 9 tests 1-8, 5 criteria PDFs, exam1, synthetic-full-1), every one fixture-only, none with a RightsProof - M-53 left unanswered per G-11. Two independent guards stop an ungrounded grant: Register throws, and Evaluate refuses such a grant even arriving from storage. The rule is one pure function that accepts null - ContentRightsPolicy.Evaluate(ContentSource?, environment, now) - so an unregistered source is a valid input whose answer is no, making default-deny true by construction rather than by an early return every caller must remember. Bound by definition id as well as version id because DevelopmentExamSeeder derives version ids from a content fingerprint, so any content edit mints a new one and a version-keyed registry would silently lose its own material. Refusal is 409 CONTENT_RIGHT_MISSING with reason and sourceId extensions, checked BEFORE the already-published check."
    },
